Stage 5: design/SHAPES.md's content sets. The string path is 174 of 413 fields,
so every number in stage3-arms.log being an ASCII number is half a number.

commit:   d281acf6ae3d78510d62c68f969ff16fe52e0768 (+ this slice)
build:    arm a, target sources, .NET 8.0.31, Google.Protobuf 3.28.3
sets:     ascii (the default and the only one ffi/schema emits);
          latin1  U+00A0 to U+00FF, two UTF-8 bytes;
          wide    U+4E00 and up, three UTF-8 bytes.

THE SETS ARE NOT PINNED BY THE DESCRIPTION, and that had a visible cost here.
ffi/schema emits ASCII only and names the others by character RANGE, and
"above U+00FF" admits both a two-byte and a three-byte encoding. The first
version of this file picked mostly two-byte and measured wide at 1.78 to 1.84
times the ASCII wire, against the Rust slice's published 2.39 to 2.50. That is
not two runtimes disagreeing, it is two slices choosing different characters,
and under R1 it is a defect rather than a difference. This run picks three
bytes throughout so the column lands where the Rust slice's did. Raised in
STATE.md as a request: agreeing by hand is what one description exists to stop.

What this prices is a NARROWING TRANSCODER. The host holds UTF-16 and on ASCII
the transcode is one char to one byte, so the transcoder has nothing to do.
The Rust slice's content-set rows price validation and width instead, because
a Rust String is already UTF-8. The two columns are not each other's comparator.

